comment A line comment, running to the end of the line.

comment begin
    A block comment. It opens with the word "comment" followed by "begin", and closes
    with the two words "end" and "comment" in that order -- reversed rather than
    repeated, so an opener and a closer never look alike.

    A block may span as many lines as it needs.

    One consequence worth knowing: a block comment cannot contain its own closing
    phrase, not even inside quotes. The scanner does not read quotes while skipping a
    comment, so those two words together always close the block, wherever they appear.
end comment

comment begin A block may also open and close on one line. end comment

global model Comments

    comment A comment may sit on its own line inside a model,

    constant integer Answer = 42;      comment or trail a declaration on the same line.

    function Placement()
        comment A comment before a statement.
        integer total = 0;

        total = total + 1;             comment A comment after one.

        comment begin
            A block comment inside a function body. The word end appearing on its own
            does not close it, and neither does the word comment on its own. Only the
            two together do.
        end comment

        if total > 0
            comment Comments nest inside any construct.
            total = total - 1;
        end if
    end function

    comment Words that merely start with the same letters are not comments, so an
    comment identifier such as commentary below is scanned normally.
    function Boundaries()
        integer commentary = 1;
        integer ending = 2;
        integer beginning = 3;
    end function

end model

comment A trailing comment at the very end of the file.
